 A JS type's declaration is its `#[rust_js::types]`; one without is `any`.
 See [Export Rust APIs to JavaScript](export-to-javascript.md).
+
+## LazyCell and LazyLock declarations
+
+An exported lazy value retains its payload type. Before initialization its
+initializer is callable, or null while initializing/poisoned; after a
+successful initialization its payload is required and the initializer is
+undefined. The declaration is the union of those storage states. An empty
+object, a wrong initializer result, and a wrong payload are rejected by
+TypeScript. Both standard lazy types use the same representation schema
+([ADR 0318](../../decisions/0318-lazy-cells.md), amended, #1752).
